{{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nSo, the pair $a=$ {{field-12}} and $b=$ {{field-13}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204214 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ede85229-ac6b-539d-a1ef-b131f6c0e688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204214 #edb/ref 1074 "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b)=(4,8)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=4,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nThen, $5a=20$ and $5b=40.$ \n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{20}_{⏟}}_{5a}≡{{40}_{⏟}}_{5b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{4}_{⏟}}_{a}≢{{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=4$ and $b=8$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204722 #edb/ref 1061 162530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f8e0e69-6710-5e49-82a0-5871fdb9f048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204723 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204723 #edb/ref 1061 162531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204723 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b4ace00c-cfe4-51a1-9841-468562e1e1f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204723 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204724 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204724 #edb/ref 1061 162532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204724 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3fcc654f-a487-5efc-84ef-833ecdb6b419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204724 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204725 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204725 #edb/ref 1061 162533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204725 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6c4ce5d-4a27-54a9-b69e-046267002d98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204726 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204726 #edb/ref 1061 162536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "feeb1738-862f-5758-bdc6-229269e60bbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204727 #edb/ref 1061 162539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204727 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c080ac90-cc06-55fb-9ed4-011f63d75f75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204727 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205223 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to disprove conditional statements. \n\nSuppose we wish to disprove a conditional statement of the following form:\n\n$$\n\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)\n$$\n\nTo disprove this statement, we must show its negation is true. \n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀x\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃x\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃x\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃x\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to disprove the implication $P\\Rightarrow Q,$ it's sufficient to find one value of the variable such that $P$ and $\\lnot Q$ are both true. This value of $x$ is a *counterexample* that disproves the original implication.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205223 #edb/ref 1065 8362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205223 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "30ca228b-fb65-5f29-b595-1982fc663520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205223 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205277 #edb/ref 1064 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n*For any $a \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ if $2 \\mid a,$ then $4 \\mid a.$*\n\nCounterexample:\n\n*To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that* \n\n$$\n2 \\mid a  \n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n4 \\not\\mid a.\n$$\n\n*Let's choose $a = 2.$ Then, we have that*\n\n$$\n2 \\mid 2\n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n4 \\not\\mid 2.\n$$\n\n*So, $a=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205277 #edb/ref 1065 9360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205277 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b019cf05-ce80-5440-9fde-c04187ec70b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205277 #edb/ref 1067 "Stating the Full Counterexample" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205279 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we wish to disprove the following statement:\n\n*For any $a \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ if $2 \\mid a,$ then $4 \\mid a$*\n\nBy denoting the predicates $P$ and $Q$ as\n\n- $P(a){:} \\quad 2 \\mid a$\n\n- $Q(a){:} \\quad 4 \\mid a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P \\Rightarrow Q.\n$$\n\nWe can disprove this statement by finding a counterexample. In other words, we need to find a value of $a$ such that its negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(2∣a)\\,∧\\,(4∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\n*To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that* \n\n$$\n2 \\mid a  \n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n4 \\not\\mid a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=2$ suffices. \n\n*Let's choose $a = 2.$ Then, we have that*\n\n$$\n2 \\mid 2\n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n4 \\not\\mid 2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\n*So, $a=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's write down the full argument." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205279 #edb/ref 1065 9362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205279 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e09d3ca0-da11-51c7-8834-d9e55a13ac5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205279 #edb/ref 1067 "Disproving a Divisibility Statement" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419242 #edb/ref 1080 "15∤6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419242 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419242 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00c3b7a9-db10-52c0-b790-08bed5ce4985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419243 #edb/ref 1080 "there exists an integer $a$ such that" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419243 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419243 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "061a239b-55c7-5ead-ab36-8bc7d7086e40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419244 #edb/ref 1080 "15∤6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419244 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419244 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06b56fba-1fe8-5018-a849-c9135a952109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419245 #edb/ref 1080 "a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419245 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17